# Animations show the point. They do not repeat his words.

**Owner law (2026-10-24):** Chris, on the Direct Book VSL: the captions already show his words. When the animation prints the same sentence, it reads like the words are being rewrapped while he talks, it is redundant, and it looks bad. "Make it a rule. Ensure no drift."

## Always

- The picture carries the point: a stamp, a gauge, a number, a list of things, a before and after.
- Any words on the animation are short (about 5 words or less per label) and say the point a **different** way than his sentence.
- Numbers, amounts, names and set terms stay exact: $32, 13 optimization points, 30 to 50 lenders, Fundhub, UnderwriteIQ.
- **No drift.** The new words mean exactly what he said. No new fact, number, promise or claim. If it can't be reworded without drift, drop the words and let the picture speak.
- Check every plate before render: no run of 4 or more words in a row that he says within 3 seconds of the animation (numbers and set terms don't count).

## Never

- Print the sentence he is saying, or most of it.
- An eyebrow that restates his line ("When a bank denies you", "The condition of your credit decides").
- A filler label that adds nothing ("Your file", "Your report").
- Reword so far that the meaning changes.

## Example

```text
He says: "When a bank denies you and never tells you why, nobody checked your file
against what that bank approves."

❌ Eyebrow "When a bank denies you", card "Your file" stamped DENIED,
   chip "Nobody checks your file against what that bank approves".
✅ Card stamped DENIED, chip "Never matched to the bank".
```

